NAME
       firewall-cmd - configure the firewalld firewall

SYNOPSIS
       firewall-cmd [--permanent] [--zone=ZONE] OPTION...
       firewall-cmd --reload

DESCRIPTION
       RHEL's firewall is the firewalld service; firewall-cmd talks to it.
       Incoming connections are allowed or refused per zone. Each network
       interface (and optionally each source address range) belongs to one
       zone, and each zone has a list of allowed services and ports.
       Anything not allowed is rejected. Outgoing traffic is not limited.

       The default zone on RHEL is public, which allows ssh, cockpit and
       dhcpv6-client. Other zones include home, work, internal, trusted
       (everything allowed), block and drop (nothing allowed). Options
       without --zone= work on the default zone.

   Runtime and permanent
       firewalld keeps two configurations:

       runtime
              What is in effect now. Changes without --permanent go here; they
              work at once but are lost at the next reload or reboot.

       permanent
              What is saved in /etc/firewalld and loaded at start and by
              --reload. Changes with --permanent go here only; they do
              nothing until you reload.

       So the usual way to make a lasting change is to add it with
       --permanent and then run firewall-cmd --reload. Alternatively make it
       in runtime, test it, and save everything with
       --runtime-to-permanent.

   Services
       A service is a name for one or more ports, defined in XML files in
       /usr/lib/firewalld/services (and your own in /etc/firewalld/services):
       http is 80/tcp, https 443/tcp, nfs 2049/tcp, mountd, rpc-bind, dns,
       ntp, samba, mysql... --get-services lists them all. Use a service name
       when there is one; use a port (8080/tcp) when there is not.

OPTIONS
   General
       --state
              Print running or not running.

       --reload
              Throw away the runtime configuration and load the permanent
              one. Existing connections stay.

       --runtime-to-permanent
              Save the whole runtime configuration as the permanent one.

       --permanent
              Work on the permanent configuration (see above).

       --zone=ZONE
              Work on ZONE instead of the default zone.

       -q, --quiet
              Do not print "success".

       --version
              Print the firewalld version.

   Zones
       --get-default-zone, --set-default-zone=ZONE
              Show or change the default zone (this change is permanent at
              once).

       --get-zones
              List all zones.

       --get-active-zones
              List the zones that have interfaces or sources, with them.

       --get-zone-of-interface=IFACE
              Which zone an interface belongs to.

       --list-all
              Everything about one zone: interfaces, sources, services,
              ports, masquerade, rich rules.

       --list-all-zones
              The same for every zone.

       --add-interface=IFACE, --change-interface=IFACE,
       --remove-interface=IFACE
              Put an interface in the zone, move it there, or take it out.

       --add-source=CIDR, --remove-source=CIDR
              Put traffic from an address range in the zone, whatever
              interface it arrives on.

       --list-interfaces, --list-sources
              Show the zone's interfaces or sources.

   Services and ports
       --add-service=SERVICE, --remove-service=SERVICE
              Allow (or stop allowing) a service.

       --query-service=SERVICE
              Is the service allowed? Prints yes or no; exit status 0 for yes.

       --list-services
              The allowed services.

       --add-port=PORT/PROTOCOL, --remove-port=PORT/PROTOCOL
              Allow a port or range, such as 8080/tcp or 5000-5010/udp.

       --query-port=PORT/PROTOCOL, --list-ports
              Check or list the allowed ports.

       --get-services
              All service names firewalld knows.

       --info-service=SERVICE
              The ports and other settings of a service.

       --new-service=NAME
              Create an empty service of your own (with --permanent).

   Other
       --add-rich-rule='RULE', --remove-rich-rule='RULE',
       --list-rich-rules
              Rules in firewalld's rich language, for example to allow a
              service only from one network:
              'rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.50.0/24" service
              name="http" accept'

       --add-masquerade, --remove-masquerade, --query-masquerade
              Turn address translation (NAT) on or off for the zone.

       --panic-on, --panic-off, --query-panic
              Drop all network traffic at once, or stop doing so.

EXAMPLES
       Allow the web server for good, and check it:

           firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http
           firewall-cmd --reload
           firewall-cmd --list-services

       Allow a container's published port 8080:

           firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8080/tcp
           firewall-cmd --reload

       See which zone enp1s0 is in and what it allows:

           firewall-cmd --get-active-zones
           firewall-cmd --list-all

       Try a change first, then keep it:

           firewall-cmd --add-service=https
           firewall-cmd --runtime-to-permanent

FILES
       /etc/firewalld/firewalld.conf
              Settings, including DefaultZone.

       /etc/firewalld/zones/*.xml
              Your saved zone changes (written by --permanent).

       /usr/lib/firewalld/services/*.xml
              The service definitions that come with firewalld.

NOTES
       If a service still cannot be reached from server2 after you opened the
       firewall, check that the service listens (ss -tlnp) and, for an
       unusual port, that SELinux allows it (semanage port -l).

       A forgotten --reload after --permanent, or a change made without
       --permanent, is the most common reason a firewall task fails after a
       reboot.

SEE ALSO
       firewall-offline-cmd(1), firewalld(1), firewalld.zones(5),
       firewalld.richlanguage(5), semanage(8), ss(8)
